(Regression test for the declaration-form unification (v0.3.6): `Create a
 T called N.` now routes every declarable type through one shared type
 resolver instead of a hardcoded `Token::Number | Token::Text | Token::
 Boolean | Token::Buffer` subset. This asserts the DEFAULT value each
 supporting type gets when Created with no initializer. `file` and `time`
 deliberately reject a bare `Create` - see tests/compile_fail/declare_
 create_file_no_initializer.vox and declare_create_time_no_initializer.vox.
 `text`'s bare default is NOT asserted here: `Create a text called n.`
 parses fine, but its codegen default-init falls through the generic
 zero/null fallback and stores a null pointer instead of a valid empty
 string, so printing it segfaults - a pre-existing codegen gap flagged
 separately, not fixed by this test. `a text called n is "".` (the typed
 is-value form) is unaffected and is covered in declare_create_is_form.vox.)

Create a number called n.
Print n.

Create a float called f.
Print f.

Create a boolean called b.
Print b.

Create a list called l.
Print l.

Create a map called m.
Print m.

Create a buffer called buf.
Print "size={buf's size}".
Print "empty={buf's empty}".

Create a value called v.
Print v.
if v is nothing, print "v is nothing".
if v is not nothing, print "v is not nothing (BUG)".

Create a timer called tm.
Print "timer created ok".
